From 5a2ddd1dfe83a5503870361b9f0680690e0ccb45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 16:46:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(android): tune for Snapdragon 8-series tablets
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four changes aimed at sustained framerate and touch feel on a flagship
Android tablet (Adreno 7xx/8xx, ~3392x2400 panel).

Render-height cap (SDL3Main.cpp). The mobile path drove the engine's
internal resolution straight from the high-density drawable, so a
OnePlus Pad 2 rendered 8.1 megapixels a frame through a 2003
fixed-function D3D8 pipeline re-emitted by DXVK. Cap the height
(default 1200, 2x the engine's 600px UI baseline) and derive the width
from the panel's true aspect so the picture still fills the screen;
DXVK's presenter scales the smaller backbuffer up. Configurable via
-renderheight, SAGE_RENDER_HEIGHT, or RenderHeight in tablet.ini, and
"native" restores the old behaviour. The comment above that block also
claimed height stayed at 600 while the code used full native — corrected.

APK asset extraction (SDL3Main.cpp). DXVK reads dxvk.conf from the
process working directory, but only fonts were ever extracted out of the
APK, so the shipped dxvk.conf was never read and every setting in it
silently fell back to desktop defaults. Added a general asset-extraction
helper and used it for dxvk.conf and tablet.ini. Neither is overwritten
once present, so on-device edits survive.

DXVK config. maxFrameLatency=1 so the selection box tracks the fingertip
instead of trailing it by queued frames; presentInterval=1 so frames the
compositor never shows stop turning into heat; numCompilerThreads=3 so
shader compilation stops flooding all eight cores mid-match; a -0.5 LOD
bias to recover apparent sharpness lost to the render-height cap. Also
documents two knobs that look useful and are traps on mobile
(hideIntegratedGraphics, maxAvailableMemory).

GameActivity. Sustained performance mode, so a long match settles at a
clock the chassis can hold rather than boosting and then falling off a
thermal cliff. Keep-screen-on, since an RTS runs long stretches with no
touch input. Cutout rendering. And system gesture exclusion on the left
and right edges: a drag-select starting at the screen edge is the same
shape as the back gesture, which otherwise throws the player out of the
match instead of selecting units.

Verified: GameActivity compiles against android.jar (API 35) and every
API guard matches the platform's own api-versions.xml. The native side
is NOT compile-verified — no NDK in this environment.
